/-! # Local ample lines on smooth groups over a normal base

Source: Raynaud, Faisceaux amples sur les schemas en groupes, Corollaire VI 2.4;
Guo, Divisors on coherent schemes and homogeneous spaces, Corollary 6.13;
Stacks Project, Morphisms, Tag 01VJ.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-normal-smooth-group-local-ample-line).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules

/-- A smooth separated group of finite presentation with connected fibres over a normal
locally Noetherian base admits ample invertible modules on an affine base open covering.
The inverse-image open is the canonical model of the base change to that open.
Normality means that each local ring is an integrally closed domain. Finite presentation
is local finite presentation and quasi-compactness; separatedness already supplies
quasi-separatedness. The last clause spells out relative ampleness over each base open,
using the same module and its canonical tensor pullback. The index type may be empty.

Source: Raynaud, Corollaire VI 2.4; Guo, Corollary 6.13; Stacks, Tag 01VJ.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-normal-smooth-group-local-ample-line).

Proof, following the six steps of the node:
1. Around each s choose an affine normal locally Noetherian base neighbourhood T.
Base change preserves smoothness, separatedness and finite presentation by
lem-pc-proper-base-change, lem-weil-pbc-separated-base-change and
lem-weil-pbc-qcqs-morphism-permanence. Choose an affine V in H_T containing e(s),
and a basic open W in T contained in e inverse-image V. Then U = V intersect H_W
is a principal open in V and is affine, as is W, by
lem-weil-pbc-affine-ideals-closed-subscheme(0). The group laws restrict by base change.
2. Each fibre is a connected finite-type group over a residue field. It is Noetherian
by lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-over-noetherian, with finitely many irreducible components
by lem-sc-noetherian-closed-subset-finite-components. By
lem-sfb-group-local-minimal-prime its local rings have unique minimal primes, so its
components are disjoint, hence open and closed. Connectedness makes the fibre
irreducible. Since U contains the identity, it is nonempty and dense in each fibre.
3. Apply lem-sfb-smooth-normal-affine-boundary-cartier to H_W and U. It makes the
reduced boundary an effective Cartier divisor D, allowing the empty divisor.
4. Put R = O(D), the dual of the invertible ideal of D
(def-pc-divisor-line-bundle, lemma-nagata-effective-cartier-is-invertible,
def-sc-invertible-module). Its canonical section is the ideal inclusion. In a local
frame generated by a non-zero-divisor f its coefficient is f, so its nonvanishing
open is exactly U.
5. Apply lem-sfb-group-affine-section-ampleness to this section with exponent one.
Its nonvanishing open is affine and meets every fibre, so R is absolutely ample.
6. Finite presentation gives quasi-compactness. Over affine W,
lem-trf-affine-base-ample-canonical-data gives ampleness after restriction to every
affine base open, which is relative ampleness. The W constructed for each s cover S.
If S is empty, choose the empty covering. -/
theorem exists_affine_cover_isAmple_of_normal_smooth_group
    (S : Scheme.{u}) [IsLocallyNoetherian S]
    (hS : ∀ s : S, IsDomain (S.presheaf.stalk s) ∧
      IsIntegrallyClosed (S.presheaf.stalk s))
    (H : Over S) [GrpObj H] [Smooth H.hom] [IsSeparated H.hom]
    [LocallyOfFinitePresentation H.hom] [QuasiCompact H.hom]
    (hconnected : ∀ s : S, ConnectedSpace (H.hom.fiber s)) :
    ∃ (I : Type u) (W : I → S.Opens),
      (∀ s : S, ∃ i : I, s ∈ W i) ∧
      ∀ i : I, IsAffineOpen (W i) ∧
        ∃ (R : (H.hom ⁻¹ᵁ W i).toScheme.Modules) (hR : R.IsInvertible),
          @IsAmple (H.hom ⁻¹ᵁ W i).toScheme R hR ∧
          ∀ V : (W i).toScheme.Opens, IsAffineOpen V →
            ∃ h : ((tensorPullback (((H.hom ∣_ W i) ⁻¹ᵁ V).ι)).obj R).IsInvertible,
              @IsAmple ((H.hom ∣_ W i) ⁻¹ᵁ V).toScheme
                ((tensorPullback (((H.hom ∣_ W i) ⁻¹ᵁ V).ι)).obj R) h := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules
